WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:04.980
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.280
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.580
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:08.820
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.640
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:19.620
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:20.780
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:27.110
Everybody and Angela, can I get a good morning?

9
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:28.370
Yeah, you can.

10
00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:29.530
Good morning, Jenna.

11
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:30.470
Good morning, Sam.

12
00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:31.550
Good morning, Cassie.

13
00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:35.770
Good morning to you out there who's just starting your day and you're listening to us in your car.

14
00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:42.090
Or good evening to my night owls who are listening maybe by the fire, maybe with a glass of wine.

15
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:42.830
I don't know.

16
00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.650
That was a beautiful picture that you just painted.

17
00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.270
Lady, guess what I did this morning?

18
00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:48.990
What?

19
00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:49.670
Something new.

20
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:50.630
I've never done it before.

21
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:51.570
Exercise.

22
00:00:59.510 --> 00:00:59.990
No.

23
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.170
But I'm so tickled by that.

24
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:07.350
I took a contrast shower.

25
00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.110
I don't know what that is.

26
00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.730
I didn't know what it was either.

27
00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:11.430
Is it a cold shower?

28
00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.630
It is an alternating hot and cold shower.

29
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:15.850
Why would you do that?

30
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.830
Okay, because it's supposed to have a bunch of health benefits.

31
00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:19.510
And you know me.

32
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:20.410
I'll try anything.

33
00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.950
You could have gotten that shower at my grandparents' house in their guest bathroom.

34
00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.510
Does it just automatically alternate for you?

35
00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:27.250
It used to.

36
00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:28.250
It's like, oh, wait, wait.

37
00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:29.450
Who just started the dishwasher?

38
00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:30.830
Don't shower.

39
00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.470
Oh, that's true at our house.

40
00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.990
Don't do a load of laundry before you get in the shower.

41
00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:36.630
Or you could have a contrast shower.

42
00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.510
Well, here's what it's supposed to do.

43
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:43.630
It's supposed to improve blood flow, reduce systemic inflammation.

44
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:48.530
It supports a connection between the brain and digestive system.

45
00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:55.370
But it's also just supposed to, like, energize you and kind of boost your immune system.

46
00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:56.330
I don't know.

47
00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:57.610
It's supposed to be beneficial.

48
00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:04.590
And it's sort of based on a thing that athletes do, you know, where they do a cold plunge and a hot shower and things.

49
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:05.970
Oh, my gosh.

50
00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:08.770
I wonder if Tom Brady does contrast showers.

51
00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:10.250
Tom Brady does it all.

52
00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:11.390
That's what I mean.

53
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:12.090
I'm telling you.

54
00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:12.870
He does it all.

55
00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.050
I should Google that.

56
00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.890
Do you think that's on the Internet?

57
00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.570
His water drinking is on the Internet?

58
00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:19.350
Okay.

59
00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:20.450
I bet it is.

60
00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:21.010
Okay.

61
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:21.930
Well, listen.

62
00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:23.010
I did it this morning.

63
00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:23.430
You know what?

64
00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:24.810
Google Tom Brady's shower.

65
00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:25.730
Oh, no.

66
00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.010
Don't Google that.

67
00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:28.530
And see what you get.

68
00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:29.130
Oh.

69
00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:29.710
Okay.

70
00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:30.270
Well.

71
00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.650
My husband's going to be like, why are you Googling?

72
00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:33.290
What is this search?

73
00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:34.850
They've given up on our search history.

74
00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:35.710
This is true.

75
00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:36.650
Wait, wait.

76
00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:37.990
Do you feel energized?

77
00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:39.630
I kind of do.

78
00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:40.410
Okay.

79
00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:41.030
I do.

80
00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:46.790
I definitely felt energized when the freezing cold water hit my body after being in warm water.

81
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.450
This would just be called grumpy shower in my house.

82
00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:53.590
I had to sort of sing it out during the cold part.

83
00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:57.630
Like when the cold water hit me, I was like, and it's cold.

84
00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.250
Yes, it's cold.

85
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:02.330
You turned into like a ye olde musical act.

86
00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:02.890
I did.

87
00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.770
And my kids were like, what is mom doing today?

88
00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:07.270
Anyway, there you go.

89
00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.230
That's what I did today.

90
00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:09.050
This morning.

91
00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.530
Did you do anything special or new or different, Ange?

92
00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.310
I did like 15 push-ups.

93
00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:14.510
This morning?

94
00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:14.870
Yeah.

95
00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.030
Before we got here.

96
00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:16.290
Yeah.

97
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.530
Do you remember John Krasinski used to do that?

98
00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:19.330
What?

99
00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:20.050
Yes.

100
00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.290
In the mornings, he would get to work.

101
00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.890
And before hair and makeup, he would do a bunch of push-ups and a bunch of sit-ups.

102
00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:26.610
Oh, my God.

103
00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.290
To energize himself for the morning.

104
00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:29.170
You don't remember that?

105
00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:29.750
No.

106
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:33.390
I was always so impressed, and I was inspired, and I thought, oh, I should do that.

107
00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.290
I tried it one morning, and I was like, I'm not doing that again.

108
00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:38.190
Well, I didn't do it either.

109
00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:38.910
I was lying.

110
00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:40.930
I was doing a joke.

111
00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.010
But geez, John.

112
00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:42.630
Wow.

113
00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:43.490
I know.

114
00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:44.990
Should we get into this episode?

115
00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:45.830
Let's do it.

116
00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.990
This episode today is Cafe Disco.

117
00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:55.430
It is Season 5, Episode 27, written by Warren Lieberstein and Halstead Sullivan,

118
00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:57.730
Randall Einhorn.

119
00:03:58.390 --> 00:03:59.530
Would you like a summary?

120
00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:00.250
Yes.

121
00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:08.250
Concerned about the serious mood of his employees and just the general drab work environment at

122
00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:15.190
Dunder Mifflin since Charles Minor left, Michael has decided to transform the former

123
00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:22.990
Michael Scott paper company into a cafe disco for socializing, dancing, and sipping espresso.

124
00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:28.950
Now, initially, nobody is interested, but it does slowly take off and ends up exceeding

125
00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.010
Michael's expectations.

126
00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:37.870
Meanwhile, Pam and Jim plan to secretly elope to Youngstown, Ohio, and Dwight tries to help

127
00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:39.030
Phyllis with an injury.

128
00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:40.290
It's a lot.

129
00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:41.530
That's a lot happening.

130
00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:42.150
That's a lot.

131
00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.510
And yet, it's a pretty simple episode, really.

132
00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:48.990
Fast fact number one was a fan question from Becca D.

133
00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.770
What was the inspiration for this

134
00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:52.250
episode?

135
00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:56.250
We reached out to writers Warren Lieberstein and Halstead Sullivan.

136
00:04:56.670 --> 00:05:00.090
Yes, Warren and Halstead were a fantastic writing team.

137
00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:03.710
I knew them for a long time from our days at the Groundlings.

138
00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:08.310
And some of you guys listening to this know, and some don't, but Warren and I used to be

139
00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:12.650
married, and we're good friends, and it was really fun to reminisce with both of them

140
00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:13.510
about this episode.

141
00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:18.630
Well, here's what he said about the inspiration for this episode, which I thought was amazing.

142
00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:22.550
He said that he and Halstead were pretty new writers at the time.

143
00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:24.510
They joined in this season.

144
00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:31.070
And they were tasked with coming up with an episode idea for what Michael Scott could do

145
00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:34.690
with this space that was formerly the Michael Scott Paper Company.

146
00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:38.690
He said that they were working in the writer's trailer with Brent Forrester, and the three

147
00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.910
of them were kind of pitching ideas like, oh, what if it's a cafe?

148
00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:43.130
What if it's a disco?

149
00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:44.810
And they couldn't really decide.

150
00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.610
He said for some reason that day, Paul Lieberstein was also working in the writer's trailer.

151
00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:51.190
He was working out of B.J.

152
00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:55.750
Novak's office that was sort of off of this communal area.

153
00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.030
So he could hear them going back and forth.

154
00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:58.710
Yes, exactly.

155
00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:04.990
And Warren said that Paul just suddenly came out of the office and said, it's a cafe and

156
00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:05.590
a disco.

157
00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:10.510
Michael Scott loves two things more than anything, coffee and dancing.

158
00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:11.510
It's a cafe disco.

159
00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:15.350
And then he turned around, went back into B.J.'s office, and shut the door.

160
00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:20.950
And Warren said he remembers Brent turning to them and saying, well, you heard him.

161
00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:26.830
And then the three of them just started outlining what is a cafe disco to Michael Scott.

162
00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:30.990
It feels very much like something your dad would do, you know, like he'd come out as

163
00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.290
you're arguing, be like, it's both.

164
00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:33.690
Just do it.

165
00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:35.130
Yes, exactly.

166
00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:38.870
Well, it is more interesting being both, isn't it?

167
00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:43.210
And it's more Michael Scott that it would be a strange hybrid of two things.

168
00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:43.890
Yes.

169
00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:46.450
Halstead had some really great stuff to share as well.

170
00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:52.490
And he told us that they won an NAACP Image Award for Best Writing for a Comedy Series for

171
00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:53.330
this episode.

172
00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:55.770
This episode is really popular.

173
00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:00.430
When I told people that we were breaking down Cafe Disco and when we were trading emails

174
00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:05.050
with Randy Cordray about this one, he was like, I love this episode.

175
00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:06.770
I love the simplicity of it.

176
00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:08.630
It's just so beautiful.

177
00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:09.410
Yeah.

178
00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:11.930
Well, Halstead also said, Ange, guess what?

179
00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:17.870
But there is a bakery in Oklahoma named Cafe Disco because they loved this episode so

180
00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:18.270
much.

181
00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:21.330
You can find them at cafe-disco.com.

182
00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:22.730
He said, have you ever heard of that?

183
00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:26.350
Have any of the other episodes had a bakery named after them?

184
00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:31.470
No, we've just heard of Shoe La La's, but I think they existed before Michael's Shoe La La.

185
00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:32.090
That's true.

186
00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:37.550
Fast fact number two, we shot this episode in four days instead of five.

187
00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:39.330
How did we do that?

188
00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:45.970
Randy shared that because all the work was so contained in that one tiny location of the

189
00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:50.770
Cafe Disco, there weren't like a ton of different ways to cover it with the camera.

190
00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:56.330
So we were able to kind of get it pretty quickly.

191
00:07:56.570 --> 00:08:00.810
So they would basically, rather than having some giant master shot and all these close-ups,

192
00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:02.710
they just covered it.

193
00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:03.170
Covered it.

194
00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:10.990
He said it was kind of important that we do this in four days because this episode filmed the week

195
00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:12.310
right before Easter.

196
00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:16.470
And we were already meant to have Good Friday off for Easter weekend.

197
00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:23.770
And if we hadn't finished, we would have had to come back the Monday after Easter and pick up our fifth day.

198
00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:30.130
But that would have really thrown off our 100th episode, Company Picnic, which was happening the next week.

199
00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:31.690
Which was a huge company move.

200
00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:32.590
That would have been...

201
00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:32.910
Yeah.

202
00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:34.270
That would have been really hard.

203
00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:34.710
Yeah.

204
00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:36.030
That's what she said.

205
00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:39.710
So that leads me into fast fact number three.

206
00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:47.310
While there were no locations for this episode, no expensive stunts or props, there was a lot of music.

207
00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:54.030
And to get the rights to all the music that was in this episode was a very complicated thing to pull off.

208
00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:56.510
Randy shared that there are two kinds of music rights.

209
00:08:56.770 --> 00:09:03.570
There are sync rights, which is where you're going to play a song by the original artist, and that's all.

210
00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:05.330
You're just going to hear their song.

211
00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:05.930
Okay.

212
00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:08.470
But then there are master rights.

213
00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:13.490
And that's what you need if a character is going to sing or hum a song.

214
00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:15.650
Sort of do their interpretation.

215
00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:16.310
Yes.

216
00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:16.870
Of the artiSt.

217
00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:23.330
And Randy said that they wanted us to be able to sing along or hum along as we were dancing.

218
00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:28.430
And so we had to get both sync and master rights to every single song.

219
00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:32.030
There was only one song that appeared in the script.

220
00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:37.290
If you read the script, different scenes will say music is playing.

221
00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:37.810
Right.

222
00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:39.210
Aaron presses play.

223
00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:42.890
But it doesn't say what song comes out except for one time in the script.

224
00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:43.690
What did it say?

225
00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:47.470
The one song that appears in the script is Everybody Dance Now.

226
00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.710
And it's the song where Michael is playing it through the vents.

227
00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:51.310
Mm-hmm.

228
00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.050
To get everybody to come down.

229
00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:56.510
And that song cost $40,000.

230
00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:57.790
Wow.

231
00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:04.970
And so Randy had our post-production supervisor, Jake Oust, reach out to Universal Music, you

232
00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:07.050
know, because our show was made by NBCUniversal.

233
00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:07.430
Right.

234
00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:12.770
And see if they could get a list of songs that we could license for either free or for not

235
00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:13.450
that much money.

236
00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:16.710
And then he gave that list to Warren and Halstead.

237
00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:18.650
And he said, what do you think?

238
00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:24.050
So Warren and Halstead made a list of, like, their dream music liSt.

239
00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:24.370
Right.

240
00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:27.470
And then next to it, they would put a free alternative.

241
00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:30.310
And Randy sent this list over.

242
00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:31.910
It was really fascinating to look at.

243
00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:33.330
I'll just give one example.

244
00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:36.850
You know that opening scene when Michael's kind of dancing alone?

245
00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:37.430
Yeah.

246
00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:41.050
Their first choice was Disturbia by Rihanna.

247
00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:41.850
Okay.

248
00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:46.350
Their free alternative, which appears in the show, is Car Wash.

249
00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:47.910
All right.

250
00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:51.970
For the most part, we had to go with their free alternative.

251
00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:52.870
I was going to say.

252
00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:54.130
I was going to say, I'm sure.

253
00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:59.550
The ultimate music budget for this episode was $85,200.

254
00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:01.070
Wow.

255
00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:05.430
Most of that money paid for Everybody Dance Now and YMCA.

256
00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:10.350
But Randy said that another perk of not having that fifth shoot day was that they were able

257
00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:14.450
to allocate some of that shoot day's budget to music.

258
